# What is committed here, and why.
#
# `fragments/` and `declaration.json` are the inputs to a committed artefact.
# The design claim this repository makes is that a change to the admitted set
# arrives as a reviewable diff -- and without the fragments you see `233 -> 232`
# in the contract and cannot see which check changed its mind. They are also
# what `stamp-contract --check` reads, so committing them is what lets CI verify
# the contract without an R toolchain or the data.
#
# `tables/` is the evidence: the per-column report somebody opens to find out
# why a column they cared about was dropped. A verdict nobody can look up is not
# evidence. About 320 KB, and the repository already had this habit.
#
# Everything else here is a rendering artefact and is regenerated by a run.
*.html
*_files/
*_cache/
